schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Donor Stewardship in Web3
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A short guide on stewarding crypto donors: thank donors within 24 hours
    (including the transaction ID as blockchain proof), provide ongoing impact
    updates (photos, stories, crypto-to-impact conversions), understand crypto
    donor motivations (transparency, global access, innovation), consider
    publishing onchain proof of fund usage, and build community around major
    crypto donors.
  audience: nonprofit/regenerative-project fundraisers (grounded-regen)
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      crypto-native donor expectation of onchain transparency vs traditional
      nonprofit donor-communication norms — the article frames this gap as an
      opportunity ("builds trust in a way traditional finance can't match")
      rather than a problem.
  related_concepts:
    - blockchain-transparency-for-nonprofits
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/donor-stewardship.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-6040541f9808
  source_lineage: donor-stewardship.md
  notes: >
    # assessed-but-excluded

    - Article's own frontmatter marks research_done: false and gives a single
      source ("CryptoAltruists Web3 Impact Toolkit") with no link — no
      resource object created for it since the article gives no URL and no
      further descriptive detail to responsibly build a resource card without
      inventing facts.
    - No people/community profiles, exact locations, or MRV/ecological claims
      in this short article. The suggestion to "publish onchain proof of how
      funds are used" / a donor address is a lightweight operational
      suggestion, not a funding/legal/governance recommendation about a
      specific organization — judged not to cross the high-risk threshold, but
      flagged here for reviewer judgment call given it touches financial
      transparency practice.
